<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Intef II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2112–2063 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Wahankh Intef II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Intef II, also called Wahankh, was a king of the Eleventh Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> for about fifty years during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>. He claimed the title of king of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, fought the rival Herakleopolitan kings for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a> and Abydos, and extended Theban control northward. His long reign prepared the reunification of Egypt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a>. He was buried in a rock-cut saff tomb at el-Tarif.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/intef-ii.jpg" alt="Intef II" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_of_King_Intef_II_Wahankh_MET_13.182.3_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
